she is actually the

00:58:37.930 --> 00:58:38.800
leader of the zombies.

00:58:38.800 --> 00:58:40.050
Oh crap.

00:58:42.610 --> 00:58:44.990
AUDIENCE: What if the
zombies are aliens?

00:58:44.990 --> 00:58:45.560
AUDIENCE: Zombie Aliens?

00:58:45.560 --> 00:58:45.970
AUDIENCE: The flood.

00:58:45.970 --> 00:58:47.410
AUDIENCE: What if the
zombies are ninjas?

00:58:49.930 --> 00:58:52.530
AUDIENCE: Combine the zombie
Pokemon idea with the Sims,

00:58:52.530 --> 00:58:54.560
where you have a zombie that's
under your control, and you're

00:58:54.560 --> 00:58:56.380
trying to teach your
zombie to love.

00:58:56.380 --> 00:58:57.693
And maybe you're a
different avatar.

00:58:57.693 --> 00:58:58.710
You're a human avatar.

00:58:58.710 --> 00:59:01.540
And you go through quests where
you take your zombie

00:59:01.540 --> 00:59:03.330
places and teach your
zombie things.

00:59:03.330 --> 00:59:04.950
AUDIENCE: Like not
to bite people.

00:59:04.950 --> 00:59:07.172
AUDIENCE: Yeah exactly.

00:59:07.172 --> 00:59:07.900
I don't know.

00:59:07.900 --> 00:59:09.990
Train your zombie.

00:59:09.990 --> 00:59:12.680
AUDIENCE: So the whole thing
with the ninja zombies, why

00:59:12.680 --> 00:59:18.370
not have pirates as well,
pirate zombies.

00:59:18.370 --> 00:59:20.100
AUDIENCE: Pirate versus
ninja zombies.

00:59:20.100 --> 00:59:20.920
AUDIENCE: Zombie edition.

00:59:20.920 --> 00:59:21.690
Special edition.

00:59:21.690 --> 00:59:25.650
AUDIENCE: In space
wrapped in bacon.

00:59:25.650 --> 00:59:26.900
AUDIENCE: Chtulu, but time
traveling zombies.

00:59:29.020 --> 00:59:31.125
AUDIENCE: It would be a great
game to make, just pirate

00:59:31.125 --> 00:59:33.740
zombies versus ninja zombies
in space wrapped in bacon.

00:59:33.740 --> 00:59:36.980
And just make that game.

00:59:36.980 --> 00:59:38.890
AUDIENCE: So, is
it necrophilia?

00:59:38.890 --> 00:59:40.140
Probably.

00:59:42.170 --> 00:59:45.090
Pirate edition.

00:59:45.090 --> 00:59:48.365
AUDIENCE: So maybe we should
divide it into little

00:59:48.365 --> 00:59:50.205
sub-brainstorms for a name,
tagline, all that.

00:59:50.205 --> 00:59:53.780
As a name, do we have any
more brainstorming?

00:59:53.780 --> 00:59:55.390
AUDIENCE: I think we
should figure out a

00:59:55.390 --> 00:59:57.900
mechanic that works.

00:59:57.900 --> 00:59:59.810
So far we have a lot of ideas
about what the game world is.

00:59:59.810 --> 01:00:02.610
We have no idea how you
play with game.

01:00:02.610 --> 01:00:04.275
AUDIENCE: So maybe more
brainstorming on that then?

01:00:09.230 --> 01:00:10.750
AUDIENCE: It's massive
multiplayer.

01:00:10.750 --> 01:00:13.772
AUDIENCE: Ones working such
as typical WoW-style RPGs,

01:00:13.772 --> 01:00:18.070
typical massive multiplayer
IPS.

01:00:18.070 --> 01:00:19.520
Pokemon style RPG.

01:00:19.520 --> 01:00:21.900
AUDIENCE: Pokemon FPS.

01:00:21.900 --> 01:00:24.030
AUDIENCE: Or, as we were saying
before, we could get

01:00:24.030 --> 01:00:24.880
The Sims online.

01:00:24.880 --> 01:00:27.152
AUDIENCE: The Sims online was
so good back in the day.

01:00:27.152 --> 01:00:28.980
AUDIENCE: Did you play
the Pokemon?

01:00:28.980 --> 01:00:29.990
AUDIENCE: Yeah, we could play
the Pokemon, but it's a first

01:00:29.990 --> 01:00:33.000
person shooter.

01:00:33.000 --> 01:00:34.810
But you still have
an RPG, first

01:00:34.810 --> 01:00:36.060
person shooter as Pokemon.

01:00:41.430 --> 01:00:42.680
AUDIENCE: It could entirely
text-based.

01:00:47.430 --> 01:00:50.300
AUDIENCE: You have to fight--
you have battles with words

01:00:50.300 --> 01:00:52.606
like Monkey Island.

01:00:52.606 --> 01:00:56.520
AUDIENCE: I like that,
text-based zombie love.

01:00:56.520 --> 01:00:57.970
AUDIENCE: Text-based
zombie love.

01:00:57.970 --> 01:00:59.760
AUDIENCE: And then he sticks his
really, really long tongue

01:00:59.760 --> 01:01:02.990
down your throat.

01:01:02.990 --> 01:01:05.150
AUDIENCE: Zombie make
out disease.

01:01:05.150 --> 01:01:06.150
AUDIENCE: I thought
of a title.

01:01:06.150 --> 01:01:08.215
AUDIENCE: If it's massively
multiplayer, what if you

01:01:08.215 --> 01:01:10.950
randomly get paired with five
other players who are playing

01:01:10.950 --> 01:01:11.360
this game, and you
all have some

01:01:11.360 --> 01:01:12.340
task you have to complete.

01:01:12.340 --> 01:01:14.290
And you are given these
particular zombie Pokemon that

01:01:14.290 --> 01:01:16.750
you can use this particular
task.

01:01:16.750 --> 01:01:19.310
So you'd be working with
those five, six people.

01:01:19.310 --> 01:01:20.170
AUDIENCE: Wait, will you
have zombie Pokeballs?

01:01:20.170 --> 01:01:20.590
AUDIENCE: What?

01:01:20.590 --> 01:01:21.300
AUDIENCE: We have a title.

01:01:21.300 --> 01:01:27.002
AUDIENCE: So Necrofeelia
with F-E-E-L.

01:01:27.002 --> 01:01:30.584
AUDIENCE: The first
zombie sex game.

01:01:30.584 --> 01:01:31.834
AUDIENCE: Exactly.

01:01:33.940 --> 01:01:35.060
AUDIENCE: And this can be
[UNINTELLIGIBLE], you have to

01:01:35.060 --> 01:01:37.060
be tentacles somewhere.

01:01:37.060 --> 01:01:38.800
AUDIENCE: What if you
are a zombie.

01:01:38.800 --> 01:01:40.350
And all the players
are zombies.

01:01:40.350 --> 01:01:43.150
And the NPCs are the humans, and
you try to go on quests to

01:01:43.150 --> 01:01:45.870
make yourself more attractive
and learn human customs.

01:01:45.870 --> 01:01:47.830
And you get points for
seducing humans.

01:01:51.570 --> 01:01:53.490
AUDIENCE: You have to make sure
your brains meter doesn't

01:01:53.490 --> 01:01:54.350
get too low.

01:01:54.350 --> 01:01:56.390
AUDIENCE: So you have
to eat them.

01:01:56.390 --> 01:01:57.910
AUDIENCE: Use them and
then eat them.

01:01:57.910 --> 01:01:59.900
AUDIENCE: And then if you eat
them with certain qualities--

01:02:02.410 --> 01:02:04.850
AUDIENCE: That actually sounds
like a fun vampire game.

01:02:04.850 --> 01:02:05.430
You're a vampire.

01:02:05.430 --> 01:02:08.525
You have to seduce a human
female to bring back to drink.

01:02:08.525 --> 01:02:09.960
AUDIENCE: It's like Prototype.

01:02:09.960 --> 01:02:11.750
AUDIENCE: Or that you have to
seduce a human because you

01:02:11.750 --> 01:02:12.820
want to seduce a human.

01:02:12.820 --> 01:02:14.180
But at the same time, you
have to eat humans.

01:02:14.180 --> 01:02:16.440
So you have to hide the fact
that you're eating from the

01:02:16.440 --> 01:02:17.280
human you're trying to seduce.

01:02:17.280 --> 01:02:17.580
AUDIENCE: Oh, I see.

01:02:17.580 --> 01:02:19.720
So you have a human girlfriend,
and then you eat

01:02:19.720 --> 01:02:20.470
other humans.

01:02:20.470 --> 01:02:22.630
AUDIENCE: While she's
not looking.

01:02:22.630 --> 01:02:25.026
AUDIENCE: Have you been eating
other girlfriends without me?

01:02:25.026 --> 01:02:26.930
Where's my stepdad?

01:02:26.930 --> 01:02:28.010
Uh, about that.

01:02:28.010 --> 01:02:29.120
AUDIENCE: Oh, that's
who that guy is.

01:02:29.120 --> 01:02:31.230
I thought he was the
delivery guy.

01:02:31.230 --> 01:02:31.570
AUDIENCE: Let's see.

01:02:31.570 --> 01:02:33.228
Zombies meets American Psycho.

01:02:36.156 --> 01:02:37.406
You've never seen
American Psycho?

01:02:41.230 --> 01:02:43.880
He goes around killing random
people, but he has a

01:02:43.880 --> 01:02:45.220
girlfriend on the side.

01:02:45.220 --> 01:02:46.425
But he's crazy.

01:02:46.425 --> 01:02:47.730
It's fun.

01:02:47.730 --> 01:02:49.220
AUDIENCE: So it's like Psycho.

01:02:49.220 --> 01:02:50.470
AUDIENCE: But better.

01:02:58.550 --> 01:03:00.660
AUDIENCE: So Necrofeelia.

01:03:00.660 --> 01:03:03.900
AUDIENCE: Get infected
with emotion.

01:03:03.900 --> 01:03:07.006
AUDIENCE: Necrofeelia,
infected with love.

01:03:07.006 --> 01:03:11.122
AUDIENCE: Brains, love,
it's the perfect game.

01:03:11.122 --> 01:03:13.026
AUDIENCE: All right guys, I'm
going to have you stop.

01:03:13.026 --> 01:03:14.620
We're going to bring
the other group in.

01:03:14.620 --> 01:03:16.730
AUDIENCE: Love after Life?

01:03:16.730 --> 01:03:17.980
AUDIENCE: I like it.

01:03:20.416 --> 01:03:21.666
AUDIENCE: That's really good.

01:03:26.340 --> 01:03:27.530
AUDIENCE: All right guys.

01:03:27.530 --> 01:03:28.290
AUDIENCE: Our publisher
is going to

01:03:28.290 --> 01:03:29.540
give us so much money.

01:03:33.890 --> 01:03:36.640
AUDIENCE: It may seem odd to
have you do this brainstorm,

01:03:36.640 --> 01:03:40.180
and then what I actually want
to talk about is rather than

01:03:40.180 --> 01:03:41.566
give [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
because I'm sure

01:03:41.566 --> 01:03:42.028
they're all really good.

01:03:42.028 --> 01:03:43.414
AUDIENCE: Ahh.

01:03:43.414 --> 01:03:44.230
ABE STEIN: I know, so sad.

01:03:44.230 --> 01:03:47.980
But what really matters is the
process, because the point of

01:03:47.980 --> 01:03:50.200
brainstorming is to
withhold judgment.

01:03:50.200 --> 01:03:54.817
Judging which of your ideas is
best shouldn't have even been

01:03:54.817 --> 01:03:55.862
happening at this stage.

01:03:55.862 --> 01:03:58.240
And so one kind of question--

01:03:58.240 --> 01:04:00.140
we'll come back to
what went well--

01:04:00.140 --> 01:04:05.360
but what sort of stumbling
blocks did you guys run into

01:04:05.360 --> 01:04:06.740
in this process as you
were doing it?

01:04:06.740 --> 01:04:07.770
Did it all go perfectly?

01:04:07.770 --> 01:04:10.265
I highly doubt it.

01:04:10.265 --> 01:04:13.956
AUDIENCE: I would say we were
trying to design this whole

01:04:13.956 --> 01:04:15.255
game, so sometimes we
would say features.

01:04:15.255 --> 01:04:16.752
And then we [INAUDIBLE].

01:04:23.239 --> 01:04:25.334
ABE STEIN: Yeah, in some senses,
it may have been

01:04:25.334 --> 01:04:25.734
constrained in one area.

01:04:25.734 --> 01:04:28.230
Sure, zombies and
a love story.

01:04:28.230 --> 01:04:30.962
But then it was asking for a
whole lot, and so it could

01:04:30.962 --> 01:04:33.208
have been, can we just
brainstorm a title.

01:04:33.208 --> 01:04:35.650
Or could we just brainstorm
this or brainstorm that.

01:04:35.650 --> 01:04:36.050
What about you?

01:04:36.050 --> 01:04:38.972
AUDIENCE: We very briefly got
sidetracked on Pokemon.

01:04:41.520 --> 01:04:45.050
For a second, it was Pokemon
only, Pokemon

01:04:45.050 --> 01:04:46.300
first person shooter.

01:04:50.070 --> 01:04:51.210
ABE STEIN: Any other thoughts?

01:04:51.210 --> 01:04:52.440
I mean you can talk about
the whole group.

01:04:52.440 --> 01:04:54.270
You can talk about your own
difficulty you had personally,

01:04:54.270 --> 01:04:58.585
whether you had any
challenges with--

01:04:58.585 --> 01:05:00.851
AUDIENCE: So I feel like if
somebody says a really good

01:05:00.851 --> 01:05:03.190
idea, then everybody is stuck on
that idea. and you have to

01:05:03.190 --> 01:05:05.070
get through that whole
idea before any other

01:05:05.070 --> 01:05:05.960
ideas can come out.

01:05:05.960 --> 01:05:09.156
ABE STEIN: Yeah, that's true.

01:05:09.156 --> 01:05:09.650
AUDIENCE: We had a
stumbling block.

01:05:09.650 --> 01:05:14.590
What other MMOs are
there out there?

01:05:14.590 --> 01:05:17.554
So [INAUDIBLE] a
few other ones.

01:05:17.554 --> 01:05:21.012
That was a dead spot in
the brainstorming.

01:05:21.012 --> 01:05:22.494
ABE STEIN: Sure.

01:05:22.494 --> 01:05:23.976
AUDIENCE: I was the scribe.

01:05:23.976 --> 01:05:28.422
It was hard to jump in and
talk about your own ideas

01:05:28.422 --> 01:05:29.800
because I was so busy writing
everyone else's.

01:05:29.800 --> 01:05:31.250
ABE STEIN: It was really hard
with brainstorming.

01:05:31.250 --> 01:05:33.316
You guys actually-- both
groups-- but I

01:05:33.316 --> 01:05:34.615
stopped in with you guys.

01:05:34.615 --> 01:05:36.001
You guys had this interesting
momentum going, and I was

01:05:36.001 --> 01:05:37.920
watching you try to
write it all down.

01:05:37.920 --> 01:05:41.340
It was way hard because the
ideas were coming in as you

01:05:41.340 --> 01:05:42.560
were finishing the last idea.

01:05:42.560 --> 01:05:47.530
So if a group has really good
momentum, I actually really

01:05:47.530 --> 01:05:50.752
like the idea of having two
people where each person is

01:05:50.752 --> 01:05:51.110
writing every other one.

01:05:51.110 --> 01:05:54.210
Of course the difficulty is then
you pull two people out

01:05:54.210 --> 01:05:54.810
of contributing.

01:05:54.810 --> 01:05:59.164
So the organization of the
actual group is important.

01:05:59.164 --> 01:06:00.788
AUDIENCE: Do you ever recommend

01:06:00.788 --> 01:06:01.950
using a tape recorder?

01:06:01.950 --> 01:06:02.600
ABE STEIN: Yeah.

01:06:02.600 --> 01:06:04.100
Yeah, absolutely.

01:06:04.100 --> 01:06:05.380
I mean it certainly
makes sense.

01:06:05.380 --> 01:06:08.924
One of the things that Osborn
says which I think is--

01:06:08.924 --> 01:06:10.780
AUDIENCE: You can't put
a tape recorder down.

01:06:10.780 --> 01:06:11.244
I remember that.

01:06:11.244 --> 01:06:13.670
ABE STEIN: Yeah, but he also
says that one of the most

01:06:13.670 --> 01:06:16.290
important things for the
secretary scribe is to number

01:06:16.290 --> 01:06:20.440
things so that you know
how many you have.

01:06:20.440 --> 01:06:23.651
Is that just to support
your work?

01:06:23.651 --> 01:06:26.453
Look, we came up
with 110 ideas.

01:06:26.453 --> 01:06:27.854
Amazing.

01:06:27.854 --> 01:06:30.090
How many doesn't seem to
matter as much as the

01:06:30.090 --> 01:06:30.540
qualities of the ideas.

01:06:30.540 --> 01:06:33.216
So I thought it was weird
that he said it.

01:06:33.216 --> 01:06:36.280
But yeah, tape recording,
video recording.

01:06:36.280 --> 01:06:38.393
Any way of documenting the
process is I think the most

01:06:38.393 --> 01:06:39.590
important part.

01:06:39.590 --> 01:06:42.160
And actually, he does make a
good point of saying that one

01:06:42.160 --> 01:06:45.066
of the problems with
brainstorming as it evolved

01:06:45.066 --> 01:06:47.667
from when he first introduced it
at this one place and then

01:06:47.667 --> 01:06:49.800
people started running with it
without thinking about the

01:06:49.800 --> 01:06:52.250
principles is they would
brainstorm, and then that was

01:06:52.250 --> 01:06:52.870
sort of it.

01:06:52.870 --> 01:06:56.240
And you really have to
look back at what

01:06:56.240 --> 01:06:57.820
you had come up with.

01:06:57.820 --> 01:07:00.476
It's really important to reflect
on what you've done as

01:07:00.476 --> 01:07:02.300
a second step.

01:07:02.300 --> 01:07:04.750
And so that's important too.

01:07:04.750 --> 01:07:08.010
How about the judgement thing?

01:07:08.010 --> 01:07:10.020
I didn't notice a whole
lot of criticism.

01:07:10.020 --> 01:07:10.370
It was really good.

01:07:10.370 --> 01:07:12.560
Nobody was like, that's
a terrible idea.

01:07:12.560 --> 01:07:15.270
But did you find it hard to not
pass judgment on ideas as

01:07:15.270 --> 01:07:16.500
they were coming out?

01:07:16.500 --> 01:07:18.468
Even your idea?

01:07:18.468 --> 01:07:21.980
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I mean I
definitely let more than one,

01:07:21.980 --> 01:07:24.590
"I like that" slips.

01:07:24.590 --> 01:07:27.350
ABE STEIN: That's OK.

01:07:27.350 --> 01:07:30.100
What you want to avoid is like
the, oh my god, we've got to

01:07:30.100 --> 01:07:31.620
do this one.

01:07:31.620 --> 01:07:35.868
That will derail everything
because then it's just a loud

01:07:35.868 --> 01:07:37.644
voice that proclaims
that we're done

01:07:37.644 --> 01:07:40.010
because we got the idea.

01:07:40.010 --> 01:07:43.180
But nothing encourages a person
who is shy more than

01:07:43.180 --> 01:07:45.250
feeling like their idea
actually was good or

01:07:45.250 --> 01:07:45.734
meritorious.

01:07:45.734 --> 01:07:49.620
And so that sort of
stuff is fine.

01:07:49.620 --> 01:07:53.170
You want to avoid the
stop the process.

01:07:53.170 --> 01:07:56.095
AUDIENCE: I think I did say at
some point, ah, we've got our

01:07:56.095 --> 01:07:57.330
title, which is probably
not good.

01:07:57.330 --> 01:07:59.628
ABE STEIN: Yeah, it's
just a hard thing to

01:07:59.628 --> 01:08:01.350
train yourself to do.

01:08:01.350 --> 01:08:02.334
AUDIENCE: Is it Necrophilia?

01:08:02.334 --> 01:08:03.810
A great title.

01:08:03.810 --> 01:08:05.800
ABE STEIN: Are there
self-censors still out there?

01:08:05.800 --> 01:08:08.244
Were there a lot of people who
thought of ideas but then just

01:08:08.244 --> 01:08:09.218
didn't contribute?

01:08:09.218 --> 01:08:11.166
Good, you didn't.

01:08:11.166 --> 01:08:15.498
It's great if everybody
felt free.

01:08:15.498 --> 01:08:18.880
Cool, that's really good.

01:08:18.880 --> 01:08:21.160
So I would say that's one
thing that went well.

01:08:21.160 --> 01:08:23.649
What other things went
well for you guys?

01:08:23.649 --> 01:08:25.870
AUDIENCE: The tagline.

01:08:25.870 --> 01:08:29.488
ABE STEIN: Specifically, just
coming up with taglines?

01:08:29.488 --> 01:08:32.149
AUDIENCE: Infected by the--

01:08:32.149 --> 01:08:34.136
Is it love after life.

01:08:34.136 --> 01:08:34.870
ABE STEIN: That's cool.

01:08:34.870 --> 01:08:36.399
Did you guys feel like
you had momentum?

01:08:36.399 --> 01:08:40.240
It seemed to me watching that
there was momentum building.

01:08:40.240 --> 01:08:42.910
AUDIENCE: I think we did a
really good job of taking a

01:08:42.910 --> 01:08:46.750
lot of different ideas that we
had and synthesizing into

01:08:46.750 --> 01:08:47.029
different things.

01:08:47.029 --> 01:08:48.390
Saying, oh, that idea would
work well if we did this.

01:08:48.390 --> 01:08:50.306
Oh, and then we could
use that name, and

01:08:50.306 --> 01:08:51.384
that would fit there.

01:08:51.384 --> 01:08:51.815
ABE STEIN: Yeah.

01:08:51.815 --> 01:08:57.010
Who were the two leaders for
the two groups, the clerks?

01:08:57.010 --> 01:09:00.080
Did you guys feel like you had
to do much facilitating.

01:09:00.080 --> 01:09:01.399
AUDIENCE: Not really, the hard
part was really at the

01:09:01.399 --> 01:09:03.489
beginning, where it was,
how do we want to

01:09:03.489 --> 01:09:04.282
approach this thing?

01:09:04.282 --> 01:09:06.819
And so I guess we decided
to start with the title.

01:09:06.819 --> 01:09:10.113
It was either the titles
or the main features.

01:09:10.113 --> 01:09:12.427
And we were just like, let's
come up with a whole bunch of

01:09:12.427 --> 01:09:14.041
titles and go from there.

01:09:14.041 --> 01:09:15.514
ABE STEIN: Sure.

01:09:15.514 --> 01:09:18.479
AUDIENCE: I forgot
I was the leader.

01:09:18.479 --> 01:09:21.420
I didn't lead anything.

01:09:21.420 --> 01:09:23.893
ABE STEIN: If you don't have a
whole lot of facilitating to

01:09:23.893 --> 01:09:26.679
do, it generally means that
the group is running OK.

01:09:26.679 --> 01:09:28.160
I do think you have
a good point.

01:09:28.160 --> 01:09:30.943
A lot of the facilitator's work
comes at the beginning of

01:09:30.943 --> 01:09:33.720
the process, or even before the
process began, making sure

01:09:33.720 --> 01:09:35.190
the constraints are clear.

01:09:35.190 --> 01:09:37.476
And then you guys know
what you are going

01:09:37.476 --> 01:09:39.110
to be working on.

01:09:39.110 --> 01:09:41.780
OK, so bringing this back--
this is a game

01:09:41.780 --> 01:09:42.020
design class, right?--

01:09:42.020 --> 01:09:43.760
I touched on it earlier.

01:09:43.760 --> 01:09:48.715
How do you guys see this as
being a useful mechanic or a

01:09:48.715 --> 01:09:50.560
useful tool You know what?

01:09:50.560 --> 01:09:51.670
It's funny I called
it a mechanic.

01:09:51.670 --> 01:09:53.674
Did any of you catch that
paragraph in Alex Osborn where

01:09:53.674 --> 01:09:56.080
the guy was saying, "When my
team feels like they're

01:09:56.080 --> 01:09:58.970
playing, it's a way more
productive brainstorm than

01:09:58.970 --> 01:10:01.840
when not." And I thought that
was really interesting because

01:10:01.840 --> 01:10:06.095
there are elements of gaminess
to this, which I think is

01:10:06.095 --> 01:10:08.080
interesting.

01:10:08.080 --> 01:10:12.069
So how do you guys this could
improve the general game

01:10:12.069 --> 01:10:13.319
design process?

01:10:16.542 --> 01:10:19.530
AUDIENCE: I feel like after
you have a game and you're

01:10:19.530 --> 01:10:23.340
testing it, and there's a major
flaw that is taking too

01:10:23.340 --> 01:10:24.184
long or something like that.

01:10:24.184 --> 01:10:27.148
And everyone sits down in a
brainstorm and says, this is

01:10:27.148 --> 01:10:28.630
exactly what our problem is.

01:10:28.630 --> 01:10:30.112
How can we fix it?

01:10:30.112 --> 01:10:33.080
ABE STEIN: Yeah, overcoming
road blocks.

01:10:33.080 --> 01:10:35.230
AUDIENCE: We never would have
come up with half the cool

01:10:35.230 --> 01:10:36.565
ideas that we came up
during the process.

01:10:36.565 --> 01:10:38.940
Just me thinking alone never
would have come up with

01:10:38.940 --> 01:10:39.655
Pokemon zombies.

01:10:39.655 --> 01:10:42.156
ABE STEIN: Sure, anyone else?

01:10:45.440 --> 01:10:48.430
AUDIENCE: Well, this is good
just in general just

01:10:48.430 --> 01:10:49.340
to think up a game.

01:10:49.340 --> 01:10:52.830
Because maybe it's like,
oh, every single

01:10:52.830 --> 01:10:53.720
game has been decided.

01:10:53.720 --> 01:10:55.360
People make games
of all types.

01:10:55.360 --> 01:10:57.400
How am I going to make
a unique game.

01:10:57.400 --> 01:10:59.440
This brainstorm is like,
hmm, those two things.

01:10:59.440 --> 01:11:00.810
I've never seen that before.

01:11:00.810 --> 01:11:01.670
That could be interesting.

01:11:01.670 --> 01:11:02.640
ABE STEIN: Yeah, and
that's a really

01:11:02.640 --> 01:11:06.204
important good point actually.

01:11:06.204 --> 01:11:09.662
The synthesis thing is actually
I think something

01:11:09.662 --> 01:11:11.885
that is really important because
we tend to see a lot

01:11:11.885 --> 01:11:13.580
of recycled mechanics.

01:11:13.580 --> 01:11:15.840
And what becomes the interesting
thing in a game

01:11:15.840 --> 01:11:19.590
oftentimes is not that it's a
completely new rule, right?

01:11:19.590 --> 01:11:22.180
It's the way that rule interacts
with the other rules

01:11:22.180 --> 01:11:23.470
in the system.

01:11:23.470 --> 01:11:26.530
And in some instances, it's also
how that rule is read and

01:11:26.530 --> 01:11:29.442
interpreted by the player, or
perhaps tends to be better in

01:11:29.442 --> 01:11:30.630
terms of other players.

01:11:30.630 --> 01:11:33.955
So yeah, I think the synthesis
is actually an interesting

01:11:33.955 --> 01:11:36.833
part of this where people can
bring a lot of different ideas

01:11:36.833 --> 01:11:37.910
from a lot of different
experiences with

01:11:37.910 --> 01:11:40.555
games to fuse ideas.

01:11:45.405 --> 01:11:47.745
AUDIENCE: When it comes to
coming up with ideas for

01:11:47.745 --> 01:11:49.122
games, the nice thing about
brainstorming is that it gets

01:11:49.122 --> 01:11:51.710
past the good idea that everyone
happens to make,

01:11:51.710 --> 01:11:56.946
which is the problem that we
sometimes have in the classes.

01:11:56.946 --> 01:12:00.418
The people will find one idea
that they like, and they will

01:12:00.418 --> 01:12:04.386
never think past it because they
were happy to find one

01:12:04.386 --> 01:12:04.900
that everybody agreed with.

01:12:04.900 --> 01:12:07.230
ABE STEIN: Yeah, and so you guys
are divided into design

01:12:07.230 --> 01:12:08.480
teams, and one of the things
I would encourage--

01:12:08.480 --> 01:12:12.210
I don't know how it's set up
schedule-wise, but try to

01:12:12.210 --> 01:12:16.304
divide the brainstorming process
from the judgment,

01:12:16.304 --> 01:12:17.690
like I said.

01:12:17.690 --> 01:12:19.080
It's true.

01:12:19.080 --> 01:12:22.580
Even if you do the brainstorm,
it can help you get over.

01:12:22.580 --> 01:12:24.540
But a lot of times, whatever
had momentum during that

01:12:24.540 --> 01:12:26.450
brainstorm or whatever got
the most yuks during that

01:12:26.450 --> 01:12:29.930
brainstorm is going to be the
popular, strong idea.

01:12:29.930 --> 01:12:31.813
And then when you come back to
it with a little space, you

01:12:31.813 --> 01:12:34.808
might be like, oh, that it
is a really good idea.

01:12:34.808 --> 01:12:37.538
But there was this other thing
that got mentioned half an

01:12:37.538 --> 01:12:38.154
hour earlier that's really
interesting.

01:12:38.154 --> 01:12:40.066
Maybe we can incorporate
that somehow.

01:12:40.066 --> 01:12:44.812
And so making the judgment
happen in a separate phase can

01:12:44.812 --> 01:12:46.230
be really important.

01:12:46.230 --> 01:12:49.220
Because it's true, creativity is
a little bit more organic.

01:12:49.220 --> 01:12:51.180
It's not necessarily a strictly

01:12:51.180 --> 01:12:52.970
logical process, right?

01:12:52.970 --> 01:12:57.714
So applying logic later can be
a good thing, I think, to the

01:12:57.714 --> 01:13:00.210
creative process.

01:13:00.210 --> 01:13:03.120
Cool, any questions about
brainstorming at all?

01:13:03.120 --> 01:13:07.700
You are incredibly enlightened
by Alex Osborn?

01:13:07.700 --> 01:13:08.950
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:13:12.110 --> 01:13:16.275
using things like Google Wave
or Google Docs where things

01:13:16.275 --> 01:13:17.745
are automatically recorded
because everyone is basically

01:13:17.745 --> 01:13:19.960
typing in their ideas
as it's happening.

01:13:19.960 --> 01:13:21.970
ABE STEIN: Yeah, and I think
it's interesting.

01:13:21.970 --> 01:13:24.654
I don't have a whole lot
of experience with--

01:13:24.654 --> 01:13:27.810
well, I should rephrase.

01:13:27.810 --> 01:13:30.280
I don't have a lot of experience
with remote

01:13:30.280 --> 01:13:35.420
brainstorming, but I one of
the games I worked on this

01:13:35.420 --> 01:13:37.270
summer was this game called
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:13:37.270 --> 01:13:40.590
where you were asked a lot
of personal questions.

01:13:40.590 --> 01:13:43.110
And so we had to think of
personal questions.

01:13:43.110 --> 01:13:45.275
So we were like, well, if you
have ideas, write them down in

01:13:45.275 --> 01:13:45.720
Google Docs.

01:13:45.720 --> 01:13:48.610
And at one point, myself and
two of the other designers

01:13:48.610 --> 01:13:49.680
were all on the Google
Doc at the same time.

01:13:49.680 --> 01:13:51.120
And you could actually
see that that sort of

01:13:51.120 --> 01:13:52.860
brainstorming would happen.

01:13:52.860 --> 01:13:54.106
Someone would type
something in.

01:13:54.106 --> 01:13:55.026
And then someone would type next
to it, ha ha, that's so

01:13:55.026 --> 01:13:58.870
funny, and then write an idea
that's related to that.

01:13:58.870 --> 01:14:01.920
And you're seeing it happen
in this digital space.

01:14:01.920 --> 01:14:05.690
So I think we've gotten so
accustomed to having these

01:14:05.690 --> 01:14:10.336
digital personalities or these
digital personas in IMing and

01:14:10.336 --> 01:14:13.682
text messaging and all that
other stuff that I think we've

01:14:13.682 --> 01:14:15.180
gotten pretty adept at typing
fast enough to get ideas out

01:14:15.180 --> 01:14:16.810
that I think it could
totally work.

01:14:16.810 --> 01:14:19.642
There's something to be said
for the momentum that gets

01:14:19.642 --> 01:14:21.106
generated in person.

01:14:21.106 --> 01:14:21.960
But yeah--

01:14:21.960 --> 01:14:22.950
AUDIENCE: You could
try both ways.

01:14:22.950 --> 01:14:23.300
ABE STEIN: Yeah, I mean
it's so funny.

01:14:23.300 --> 01:14:24.051
I was going to say, no that's
a terrible idea.

01:14:24.051 --> 01:14:25.033
You have to be around
each other.

01:14:25.033 --> 01:14:28.470
And I was like, actually,
it works.

01:14:28.470 --> 01:14:29.287
I think it all depends
a little bit on the

01:14:29.287 --> 01:14:32.400
personalities of the people
that were involved.

01:14:32.400 --> 01:14:34.950
Certainly for a prolonged
brainstorm--

01:14:34.950 --> 01:14:38.450
one of the thing Alex Osborn
says is time box it.

01:14:38.450 --> 01:14:40.450
If you don't time box it, it's
going to be a problem.

01:14:40.450 --> 01:14:42.990
I think for in person,
that's true.

01:14:42.990 --> 01:14:47.320
It's so easy to get onto a
Google document now whenever

01:14:47.320 --> 01:14:50.680
you have an idea that actually
having a long form brainstorm

01:14:50.680 --> 01:14:52.690
where anytime someone has an
idea over a week or something

01:14:52.690 --> 01:14:55.060
like that, write it down.

01:14:55.060 --> 01:14:57.850
That's also useful too because
then you separated the process

01:14:57.850 --> 01:15:01.290
into collecting and--

01:15:01.290 --> 01:15:04.230
But you still should do that,
building on ideas.

01:15:04.230 --> 01:15:07.810
AUDIENCE: It's probably easier
just to do this [INAUDIBLE]

01:15:07.810 --> 01:15:11.650
when you're simultaneously
online or in the same room.

01:15:11.650 --> 01:15:14.626
ABE STEIN: Yeah, and to
see the ideas come out

01:15:14.626 --> 01:15:15.980
as they come out.

01:15:15.980 --> 01:15:20.628
Because it's a little uncommon
to just read something and be

01:15:20.628 --> 01:15:23.710
like, oh yeah, what about this
when it first shows up.

01:15:23.710 --> 01:15:27.141
Or when it first comes into
existence as you're trying to

01:15:27.141 --> 01:15:30.001
think of things that
can spur ideas.

01:15:30.001 --> 01:15:33.258
AUDIENCE: The other danger about
doing it like, OK, this

01:15:33.258 --> 01:15:35.505
document is open for the next 24
hours, everyone throw your

01:15:35.505 --> 01:15:38.106
ideas in, is that then people
start spending too

01:15:38.106 --> 01:15:39.300
long on any one idea.

01:15:39.300 --> 01:15:40.928
They start thinking about what
they're going to write down

01:15:40.928 --> 01:15:42.184
before they write it down.

01:15:42.184 --> 01:15:44.539
No, that's not the point.

01:15:44.539 --> 01:15:46.700
ABE STEIN: Yeah, think of the
paper clip experience, and

01:15:46.700 --> 01:15:48.790
those of you that raised your
hand for saying that you were

01:15:48.790 --> 01:15:49.860
self-censoring.

01:15:49.860 --> 01:15:51.450
On your own--

01:15:51.450 --> 01:15:54.196
I wasn't going to make you all
hand in those paper clip ideas

01:15:54.196 --> 01:15:55.005
you were self-censoring.

01:15:55.005 --> 01:15:57.430
So the same sort of
thing applies.

01:15:57.430 --> 01:16:01.800
You might censor yourself or
spend a long time on ideas.

01:16:01.800 --> 01:16:04.566
Any other thoughts, questions?

01:16:04.566 --> 01:16:06.425
Cool, well I hope this
was helpful.

01:16:06.425 --> 01:16:09.185
I hope it'll be helpful for
your game design stuff.

01:16:09.185 --> 01:16:12.071
Email me if you do have any
questions about this stuff.

01:16:12.071 --> 01:16:13.321
I can try to help out.

01:16:16.400 --> 01:16:19.980
Other than than, good luck on
your game design on this class

01:16:19.980 --> 01:16:21.900
and for the rest of
the semester.

01:16:21.900 --> 01:16:22.860
Cool.

01:16:22.860 --> 01:16:25.740
[APPLAUSE]

01:16:25.740 --> 01:16:28.960
ABE STEIN: I do like that of
the keynote things it says,

01:16:28.960 --> 01:16:34.730
"End of show."

01:16:34.730 --> 01:16:35.690
PROFESSOR: Let's see now.

01:16:35.690 --> 01:16:39.884
We're going to play
some card games.

01:16:39.884 --> 01:16:41.380
But before that, just a
little bit of admin.

01:16:41.380 --> 01:16:44.706
On Monday, you'll notice that
we have two readings, one of

01:16:44.706 --> 01:16:45.956
which you've already done.

01:16:49.280 --> 01:16:51.076
As for the other reading,
[UNINTELLIGIBLE], it's a

01:16:51.076 --> 01:16:52.710
little bit dry.

01:16:52.710 --> 01:16:55.650
Tough it out caffeinate
yourselves before reading it.

01:16:55.650 --> 01:16:56.630
Tough it out.

01:16:56.630 --> 01:16:58.590
It is worth it.

01:16:58.590 --> 01:17:00.440
It's a translation.

01:17:00.440 --> 01:17:01.819
Let's see now.

01:17:06.220 --> 01:17:11.191
There's at least one person who
hasn't filled a form that

01:17:11.191 --> 01:17:11.599
you need to fill out.

01:17:11.599 --> 01:17:17.467
So just come up here
and fill that out.

01:17:17.467 --> 01:17:20.190
Is Courtney in class today?

01:17:20.190 --> 01:17:21.440
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:17:23.290 --> 01:17:26.266
PROFESSOR: Yeah, I'm not sure if
we have enough time to get

01:17:26.266 --> 01:17:26.580
through all of them.

01:17:26.580 --> 01:17:29.148
But most of them are
pretty short.

01:17:29.148 --> 01:17:33.116
A lot of them today are going
to be set up, which is why

01:17:33.116 --> 01:17:35.265
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] is first
because that takes a

01:17:35.265 --> 01:17:36.588
while to set up.

01:17:36.588 --> 01:17:40.060
That being said, if you do get
a chance to look at the rules

01:17:40.060 --> 01:17:45.516
and study the cards,
even [INAUDIBLE]

01:17:45.516 --> 01:17:47.996
a bunch of smart things
[INAUDIBLE]

01:17:47.996 --> 01:17:50.020
easier to set up.

01:17:50.020 --> 01:17:53.102
And you really want to pay
attention to that.

01:17:53.102 --> 01:17:57.856
But the name of the game today
is pretty much variety.

01:17:57.856 --> 01:18:02.180
We have all fairly different
games that use cards in a very

01:18:02.180 --> 01:18:02.780
different way.

01:18:02.780 --> 01:18:04.880
And if you don't get a chance
necessarily to play all of

01:18:04.880 --> 01:18:07.280
them, at least get a chance
to look at other

01:18:07.280 --> 01:18:08.180
people playing them.

01:18:08.180 --> 01:18:10.130
And if you wanted to check them
out over the middle of

01:18:10.130 --> 01:18:12.380
the week, we'll keep them
around the office.

01:18:12.380 --> 01:18:15.980
But there'll be time to come
back here next week and

01:18:15.980 --> 01:18:16.880
[INAUDIBLE].

01:18:16.880 --> 01:18:18.680
But that's basically it.

01:18:18.680 --> 01:18:21.680
It we can clear the tables
because that's for the cards.